.SS custodian setup
.nf
usage: custodian setup [\-h] [\-\-with COMPONENTS] [\-\-profile PROFILE] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-skip\-configure]

Detects a local Hermes Agent install and orchestrates `pip install` for the components you want, so
you don't need to learn multiple package names. Bare `custodian setup` only detects and reports —
pass \-\-with or \-\-profile to actually install something.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help         show this help message and exit
  \-\-with COMPONENTS  Comma\-separated components to install, e.g. \-\-with talaria
  \-\-profile PROFILE  Install a named bundle of components (choices: hermes, minimal)
  \-\-dry\-run          Show what would be installed, do nothing
  \-\-skip\-configure   Install packages only; do not install or enable the Hermes plugin
.fi
.SS custodian doctor
.nf
usage: custodian doctor [\-h] [\-\-profile {hermes}]

options:
  \-h, \-\-help          show this help message and exit
  \-\-profile {hermes}  Require every component for this integration profile
.fi
.SS custodian init
.nf
usage: custodian init [\-h] [\-\-dir DIR] [\-\-session\-cap USD]

Create a workspace directory with a policy.yaml, state store, and README.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help         show this help message and exit
  \-\-dir DIR          Target directory (default: current directory)
  \-\-session\-cap USD  Total spend allowed per session (default: $10.00). Bands in policy.yaml set the
                     PER\-ACTION cap; the session budget has no policy field, so it is set here.
.fi
.SS custodian validate
.nf
usage: custodian validate [\-h] policy_path

Parse and validate a policy YAML. Exits 0 if valid, 1 with errors if not.

positional arguments:
  policy_path  Path to policy YAML file

options:
  \-h, \-\-help   show this help message and exit
.fi
.SS custodian status
.nf
usage: custodian status [\-h] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR]

Print current spend totals, kill\-switch state, and active policy caps.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian status\-banner
.nf
usage: custodian status\-banner [\-h] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR]

Compact dashboard view. Shows session spend, kill\-switch state, policy caps, and the 5 most recent
audit entries in a single screen.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian audit
.nf
usage: custodian audit [\-h] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR] [\-\-limit LIMIT] [\-\-event EVENT]

Print the kernel's append\-only audit trail. Every request, approval, denial, kill\-switch event, and
claim\-verification result is recorded here.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
  \-\-limit LIMIT         Max entries to show (default: 50)
  \-\-event EVENT         Filter by event type (e.g. executed, escalated, denied)
.fi
.SS custodian backup
.nf
usage: custodian backup [\-h] [\-\-force] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR] [\-\-policy POLICY] [dest]

Bundle policy.yaml and the state directory into a single .zip. The database is snapshotted with
SQLite's online\-backup API, so the backup is consistent even if the kernel is mid\-write. Credentials
are NOT in here — they live in the paladin vault (see `paladin backup`).

positional arguments:
  dest                  Destination file or directory (default: ~/custodian\-backups/custodian\-
                        backup\-<time>.zip)

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-force               Overwrite the destination file if it exists
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
  \-\-policy POLICY       Path to policy YAML file
.fi
.SS custodian restore
.nf
usage: custodian restore [\-h] [\-\-force] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR] [\-\-policy POLICY] source

Rebuild policy.yaml and the state directory from a backup .zip — here or on a brand\-new machine. An
existing workspace is never silently replaced: without \-\-force this refuses, and with \-\-force the
current files are first saved to a pre\-restore\-<time>.zip.

positional arguments:
  source                The backup .zip created by `custodian backup`

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-force               Replace the existing workspace (after saving it to a pre\-restore\-<time>.zip)
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
  \-\-policy POLICY       Path to policy YAML file
.fi
.SS custodian request
.nf
usage: custodian request [\-h] \-\-amount AMOUNT \-\-description DESCRIPTION [\-\-skill SKILL]
                         [\-\-context FLAG=true|false] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR] [\-\-policy POLICY]

The primary kernel entry point. The agent calls this; the kernel checks the amount against the
policy, the daily envelope, the kill switch, and the authority band. Returns AUTONOMOUS (proceed) or
ESCALATED (wait for human approval via Twilio Verify).

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-amount AMOUNT       Amount in USD to request
  \-\-description DESCRIPTION
                        Human\-readable description of the spend
  \-\-skill SKILL         Skill name for policy rule matching (optional)
  \-\-context FLAG=true|false
                        Context flag for rule matching (repeatable, e.g. \-\-context critical=true)
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
  \-\-policy POLICY       Path to policy YAML file
.fi
.SS custodian approve
.nf
usage: custodian approve [\-h] \-\-approved\-by APPROVED_BY [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR] code

When a request exceeds the autonomous cap, the kernel sends a Twilio Verify SMS to the operator. The
operator runs this command with the received code. The agent never sees the code.

positional arguments:
  code                  Verification code received via SMS

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-approved\-by APPROVED_BY
                        Name or ID of the approving operator
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian deny
.nf
usage: custodian deny [\-h] \-\-denied\-by DENIED_BY [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR]

Reject a pending escalation. The requesting agent receives a denial verdict.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-denied\-by DENIED_BY
                        Name or ID of the denying operator
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian kill
.nf
usage: custodian kill [\-h] \-\-by BY [\-\-reason REASON] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR]

Operator\-only emergency stop. Once engaged, every custodian request returns DENIED regardless of
amount, band, or policy. The agent cannot detect or bypass the kill switch — enforcement is in the
kernel layer, outside the agent's own process.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-by BY               Name or ID of the operator engaging the kill switch
  \-\-reason REASON       Reason for engaging (recorded in audit log)
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian resume
.nf
usage: custodian resume [\-h] \-\-by BY [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR]

Release a previously engaged kill switch. Requests are evaluated normally again.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-by BY               Name or ID of the operator releasing the kill switch
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian confirm
.nf
usage: custodian confirm [\-h] [\-\-deadline SECONDS] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR] request_id

Mark a completed action as VERIFIED or UNVERIFIED. Must be called within 60 seconds of the request
being approved.

positional arguments:
  request_id            The request\-id recorded in the audit log for this action

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-deadline SECONDS    Confirmation window in seconds (default: 60)
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian beancount
.nf
usage: custodian beancount [\-h] [\-\-since SINCE] [\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR]

Export all kernel\-verified transactions to Beancount double\-entry accounting format. Finance teams
can import this into Fava or any Beancount\-compatible tool for reconciliation.

options:
  \-h, \-\-help            show this help message and exit
  \-\-since SINCE         Only export entries on or after this date (YYYY\-MM\-DD)
  \-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR
                        Path to kernel state directory
.fi
.SS custodian tools
.nf
usage: custodian tools [\-h] {list,run,summary} ...

Custodian ships a library of governed tools. Every tool declares a custodian\-band (L0–L4) in its
SKILL.md frontmatter. The kernel checks band, spend caps, and kill\-switch state before any tool
executes. Tools with missing credentials return {ok: false, stub: true} — the framework runs without
any env vars configured.

positional arguments:
  {list,run,summary}
    list              List all registered tools grouped by authority band
    run               Invoke a governed tool by name
    summary           Print tool count and band breakdown as JSON

options:
  \-h, \-\-help          show this help message and exit
.fi
